From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Jordan <scott@spaceshipcreative.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 05:26:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 13/25] Use a Triton sparse MLA kernel for SM90-backend
 prefills on SM12x

Source: knapcio/GLM-5.3-Flash-4x-DGX-Spark-TP4 at 770d115,
overlay/glm_sparse_mla_kernel.py (kernel, SPDX Apache-2.0; the
repository is MIT) and the dispatch rules of
overlay/glm_sparse_mla_prefill.py, by knapcio. Idea and GB10
measurements: Matt Mastracci (mmastrac), GLM-5.3-Flash 4x GX10 recipe
PR #4; reproduction and ablations: chuck-ads.
Upstream status: absent (no vLLM PR).

On SM12x the SM90 sparse backend runs FlashInfer's FA2 MLA kernel, which
the source measured at 42-50 ms per DSA layer for a 6912-row prefill
chunk on one GB10 (16 heads, top-k 2048, fp8 cache). The Triton kernel
took 13-15 ms for the same chunk with the same error against an fp32
reference. One program per query row keeps every head's q in
registers, maps request-local top-k ids to cache rows through the block
table itself (no convert kernel, no plan) and stops at the row's last
valid column.

forward_mqa takes the kernel for every row of a step whose q still
holds prefill rows, i.e. prefills longer than the top-k that dense MHA
does not take, outside CUDA-graph capture, for NoPE MLA with
kv_lora_rank 512, 16/32/64 heads per rank and no DCP. Decode-only and
captured steps stay on FlashInfer. A failed launch logs a warning and
returns every layer to FlashInfer for the rest of the process.

VLLM_TRITON_SPARSE_MLA_PREFILL: unset means on when the backend runs on
SM12x, 0 forces FlashInfer, 1 asks for it explicitly.
VLLM_TRITON_SPARSE_MLA_PREFILL_CFG="BN,warps,stages[,split[,skip]]"
overrides the tile config (default 64,4,1,1, tuned at 16 heads).

The kernel and launcher are the source's, reformatted to the vLLM line
length and importing triton through vllm.triton_utils; their AST is
unchanged. The source's install hooks, drift guard, A/B harness hooks,
debug cross-check and fp32 reference are not carried.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
